WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.553
Right now, as you listen to my voice, roughly four

2
00:00:02.553 --> 00:00:05.106
billion pulses of light are rocketing through strands of glass

3
00:00:05.106 --> 00:00:07.659
beneath the Atlantic Ocean. Every credit card swipe, every flight

4
00:00:07.659 --> 00:00:10.212
path, every automated pacemaker update is riding on an invisible

5
00:00:10.212 --> 00:00:12.000
lattice of protocols written in the 1980s.

6
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.176
We treat the internet like the weather: a permanent, atmospheric

7
00:00:16.176 --> 00:00:19.099
fact of nature. But it isn't nature.

8
00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:23.281
It is glass, copper, electricity, and a handful of routing

9
00:00:23.281 --> 00:00:27.463
tables that we trust with civilization. Now imagine it is

10
00:00:27.463 --> 00:00:28.299
midnight UTC.

11
00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:32.466
Somewhere in the deep Pacific, an anomaly cascades. Within ninety

12
00:00:32.466 --> 00:00:36.633
seconds, the Border Gateway Protocol keepalive timers across twelve thousand

13
00:00:36.633 --> 00:00:38.299
autonomous systems hit zero.

14
00:00:38.299 --> 00:00:42.466
No sirens sound in the streets. No explosions light up

15
00:00:42.466 --> 00:00:43.299
the skyline.

16
00:00:43.299 --> 00:00:47.476
The first hour looks innocent enough. You wake up, reach

17
00:00:47.476 --> 00:00:50.399
for your bedside table, and check notifications.

18
00:00:50.399 --> 00:00:53.899
Nothing. You toggle airplane mode on and off.

19
00:00:53.899 --> 00:00:58.073
You cycle your home Wi-Fi router. Across the globe, four

20
00:00:58.073 --> 00:01:02.247
billion people are doing the exact same thing at the

21
00:01:02.247 --> 00:01:03.499
exact same second.

22
00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:07.648
And that harmless personal troubleshooting creates an immediate invisible catastrophe:

23
00:01:07.648 --> 00:01:11.796
a signaling storm. Every smartphone on the planet begins screaming

24
00:01:11.796 --> 00:01:14.700
reconnect handshakes into the nearest cellular tower.

25
00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:18.866
Base stations are designed to handle traffic spikes, but not

26
00:01:18.866 --> 00:01:23.033
infinite simultaneous connection requests. Worse, modern cellular towers do not

27
00:01:23.033 --> 00:01:24.700
operate in a vacuum.

28
00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:28.890
By hour three, morning arrives in London, Frankfurt, and New

29
00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:33.080
York. People leave their silent houses and step into the

30
00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:33.500
street.

31
00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:37.673
You decide to buy a coffee, or fill your car's

32
00:01:37.673 --> 00:01:41.847
gas tank before heading to work. You tap your card

33
00:01:41.847 --> 00:01:43.099
against the terminal.

34
00:01:43.099 --> 00:01:46.970
The terminal beeps, hangs for twenty seconds, and displays a

35
00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:50.841
single word: TIMEOUT. Here is the dirty secret of modern

36
00:01:50.841 --> 00:01:54.712
money: physical cash represents less than eight percent of global

37
00:01:54.712 --> 00:01:55.099
currency.

38
00:01:55.099 --> 00:01:57.957
The rest is numbers living on distributed ledger mainframes. Every

39
00:01:57.957 --> 00:02:00.814
time you buy a sandwich, your point-of-sale device initiates an

40
00:02:00.814 --> 00:02:03.671
encrypted handshake through an acquiring bank, into Visa or Mastercard's

41
00:02:03.671 --> 00:02:06.528
global switching center, and down to the Federal Reserve's Fedwire

42
00:02:06.528 --> 00:02:07.099
settlement architecture.

43
00:02:07.099 --> 00:02:11.099
When packet routing dies, the credit card terminal becomes an

44
00:02:11.099 --> 00:02:15.099
expensive plastic paperweight. Gas station pumps freeze because their automated

45
00:02:15.099 --> 00:02:19.099
safety cutoff systems require cloud telemetry authorization before releasing fuel.

46
00:02:19.099 --> 00:02:23.266
By hour six, the paralysis reaches the arteries of industrial

47
00:02:23.266 --> 00:02:27.433
logistics. Walk into an Amazon fulfillment center or an automated

48
00:02:27.433 --> 00:02:29.099
logistics depot in Rotterdam.

49
00:02:29.099 --> 00:02:33.253
Tens of thousands of autonomous orange drive units navigate by

50
00:02:33.253 --> 00:02:37.407
querying central cloud schedulers thirty times a second. Without that

51
00:02:37.407 --> 00:02:39.900
central heartbeat, emergency fail-safe brakes engage.

52
00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:44.073
Three million square feet of merchandise freeze instantly in place.

53
00:02:44.073 --> 00:02:48.247
Outside, long-haul truckers cannot receive their digital bills of lading

54
00:02:48.247 --> 00:02:49.500
or dispatch manifests.

55
00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:53.690
At the world's deepest container ports—Singapore, Shanghai, Los Angeles—giant ship-to-shore

56
00:02:53.690 --> 00:02:57.880
gantry cranes halt. Modern container vessels carry twenty-four thousand steel

57
00:02:57.880 --> 00:02:58.300
boxes.

58
00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:02.300
No human being on earth knows which box holds insulin

59
00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:06.300
and which holds plastic toys without querying an online relational

60
00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:10.300
database. Meanwhile, up in the sky, a strange drama unfolds.

61
00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:13.829
Twelve hours into the silence, the crisis moves into our

62
00:03:13.829 --> 00:03:17.358
most critical life support: the electrical grid. Most people assume

63
00:03:17.358 --> 00:03:20.888
power plants burn coal, gas, or uranium and push electricity

64
00:03:20.888 --> 00:03:22.300
down copper wires automatically.

65
00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:26.466
In reality, an electrical grid is a high-speed balancing act.

66
00:03:26.466 --> 00:03:30.633
Supply must equal demand across sixty cycles per second, every

67
00:03:30.633 --> 00:03:32.299
second of the day.

68
00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:36.050
To balance that delicate equilibrium, regional transmission operators rely on

69
00:03:36.050 --> 00:03:39.800
SCADA systems: Supervisory Control and Data Acquisition. Substations thousands of

70
00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:43.550
miles away report voltage fluctuations and phase angles across dedicated

71
00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:44.300
IP networks.

72
00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:48.478
When the digital link is severed, control room operators in

73
00:03:48.478 --> 00:03:52.657
cities across the world are suddenly flying blind. They can

74
00:03:52.657 --> 00:03:56.000
no longer read the temperatures of extra-high-voltage transformers.

75
00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:00.166
To prevent catastrophic grid-wide collapse, automated protective relays do what

76
00:04:00.166 --> 00:04:04.333
they were designed to do: they disconnect. The grid fractures

77
00:04:04.333 --> 00:04:05.999
into isolated regional islands.

78
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:09.500
Eighteen hours in. The sun has set.

79
00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:13.678
And an eerie, fascinating transformation occurs across human civilization. With

80
00:04:13.678 --> 00:04:17.857
no social media feeds, no streaming platforms, and no endless

81
00:04:17.857 --> 00:04:21.199
notifications, the noise of the digital age vanishes.

82
00:04:21.199 --> 00:04:25.349
People walk outside onto their front porches. Neighbors who haven't

83
00:04:25.349 --> 00:04:29.499
spoken in five years lean across fences to exchange news.

84
00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:33.500
In city squares, municipal workers pull out battery-powered megaphones and

85
00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:37.500
staple handwritten notices to wooden corkboards. Old dust-covered AM and

86
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:41.500
FM transistor radios are pulled from basements and attic boxes.

87
00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:44.227
Broadcast radio towers—transmitting powerful analog electromagnetic waves that require no

88
00:04:44.227 --> 00:04:46.954
IP routing or server handshakes—become the sole beacons of truth.

89
00:04:46.954 --> 00:04:49.681
Local radio hosts read lists of which grocery stores are

90
00:04:49.681 --> 00:04:52.409
accepting paper cash, which clinics have emergency power, and where

91
00:04:52.409 --> 00:04:53.500
clean water is available.

92
00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:57.681
Twenty-four hours after the blackout began, the root cause is

93
00:04:57.681 --> 00:05:01.863
resolved. Engineers in subterranean carrier hotels manually restore the BGP

94
00:05:01.863 --> 00:05:02.699
routing tables.

95
00:05:02.699 --> 00:05:06.881
Subsea amplifiers stabilize. DNS root servers begin answering queries once

96
00:05:06.881 --> 00:05:07.300
more.

97
00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:11.473
You would think that the moment the switch is flipped,

98
00:05:11.473 --> 00:05:15.647
the world simply resumes as if waking from a dream.

99
00:05:15.647 --> 00:05:16.899
Engineers know better.

100
00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:19.566
In computer science, there is a legendary nightmare known as

101
00:05:19.566 --> 00:05:22.233
the Thundering Herd Problem. For twenty-four hours, two billion automated

102
00:05:22.233 --> 00:05:24.900
IoT devices, three billion smartphones, millions of microservices, and hundreds

103
00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:27.566
of thousands of corporate cron jobs have been queueing failed

104
00:05:27.566 --> 00:05:28.900
requests with aggressive retry timers.

105
00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:32.429
The instant connectivity blinks back online, those billions of devices

106
00:05:32.429 --> 00:05:35.958
do not politely take turns. They slam every server on

107
00:05:35.958 --> 00:05:39.488
the planet with the largest distributed denial-of-service attack in the

108
00:05:39.488 --> 00:05:40.899
history of the universe.

109
00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:44.900
Twenty-four hours without the internet would cost the global economy

110
00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:48.900
an estimated fifty billion dollars in lost commerce. Millions of

111
00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:52.900
tons of perishable food would spoil in delayed refrigerated trucks.

112
00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:57.053
Contracts would default, financial settlements would tangle in courts for

113
00:05:57.053 --> 00:06:01.207
months, and supply chains would stutter for weeks. Yet, the

114
00:06:01.207 --> 00:06:03.699
greatest cost would not be financial.

115
00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:07.853
It would be psychological. Because for twenty-four hours, the veil

116
00:06:07.853 --> 00:06:09.100
would be lifted.

117
00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:10.814
We would be reminded that our modern, frictionless world—where groceries

118
00:06:10.814 --> 00:06:12.528
arrive at our door with a tap, where money exists

119
00:06:12.528 --> 00:06:14.242
as clean digital typography, and where every human being is

120
00:06:14.242 --> 00:06:15.957
reachable in milliseconds—is built on a foundation of fragile physical

121
00:06:15.957 --> 00:06:17.671
glass. A world where four billion people can be severed

122
00:06:17.671 --> 00:06:19.385
from civilization not by an asteroid or an earthquake, but

123
00:06:19.385 --> 00:06:21.100
by a corrupted routing table or a severed subsea line.

124
00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:25.300
The internet is not a cloud. It is a machine.
